<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haitian Revolution</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1791">1791</a>–<a href="/years/1804">1804</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Haitian Revolution of 1791–1804 was an uprising of enslaved people in the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> that led to the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> there and the creation of the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>. It was the only successful large-scale slave revolt to found a state. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a> it meant the loss of its richest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> colony.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution.jpg" alt="Haitian Revolution" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aaron Martinet / Alphonse-Charles Masson · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Incendie_de_la_Plaine_du_Cap._Massacre_des_Blancs_par_les_esclaves_noirs_r%C3%A9volt%C3%A9s._France_militaire._Martinet_et_Masson.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Haitian Revolution</b> of 1791 to 1804, which destroyed Saint-Domingue and produced the first Black republic in history under the leadership of Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines, was itself a direct consequence of the first empire's plantation economy and a transformative moment in Atlantic history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its support for Simón Bolívar helped the liberation of Venezuela and northern South America, and Pétion's request that Bolívar abolish slavery linked the <b>Haitian Revolution</b> to emancipation on the mainland, although the new Spanish American republics did not invite Haiti to the Congress of Panama in 1826.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Many Vodouists took part in the <b>Haitian Revolution</b> of 1791 to 1801.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Quaker and evangelical campaigners, Black writers and the enslaved themselves, through petitions, boycotts, escapes and revolts such as the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, forced governments to act, though freedom rarely brought the formerly enslaved land, compensation or equal rights.</blockquote><a href="/movement/abolitionism">Abolitionism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1903, to mark the 100th anniversary of the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, a competition was held for a national anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-dessalinienne">La Dessalinienne</a></li><li><blockquote>Enslaved Black insurgents won a victory at Croix-des-Bouquets in the first major battle of the <b>Haitian Revolution</b>, their uprising against French colonial rule in Saint-Domingue.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
